1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,000
 Hello, and welcome to our first real lecture of Internet history, technology, and security.

2
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:09,000
 We're going to start, of course, with Internet history, and I'm Charles Severance, and I'm your instructor.

3
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:12,000
 We're going to start at the dawn of electronic computing.

4
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:19,000
 Computing started early with abacuses and humans, but we're going to start with the moment of electronic computing,

5
00:00:19,000 --> 00:00:27,000
 in particular because it was the moment where computing and communications were sort of co-born at the same time.

6
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:34,000
 Then communication before the Internet became normal, then early Internet research,

7
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:41,000
 and then the Internet itself that was academia, and then, of course, went out into the real world,

8
00:00:41,000 --> 00:00:46,000
 and then the Web, which really took all this connectivity and made it easy to use for everybody.

9
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:50,000
 It's really our view now of this network is very much through the Web.

10
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:56,000
 And then from that point forward, we look at sort of the commercialization of it and the ubiquity of it

11
00:00:56,000 --> 00:01:01,000
 and the widespread use of it.

12
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:07,000
 So the first video that I want to show you, it's a little longer than most of the other videos.

13
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:13,000
 It is about Bletchley Park, and there are many heroes at Bletchley Park.

14
00:01:13,000 --> 00:01:20,000
 Of course, this was a top-secret code-breaking effort by the British government during World War II.

15
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:27,000
 World War II, I mean, if you think about it in history, perhaps there's no time in history quite like World War II.

16
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:35,000
 If you go back to what kind of technologies were using in 1910 and 1920 to the technologies we were using in 1940,

17
00:01:35,000 --> 00:01:41,000
 it's an amazing difference. Jet airplanes, radio, radar, so many things were invented

18
00:01:41,000 --> 00:01:47,000
 and made usable and made production quality during that period of time.

19
00:01:47,000 --> 00:01:53,000
 The war, of course, is terrible, but it does cause governments to fear for their lives

20
00:01:53,000 --> 00:01:57,000
 and invest heavily, very heavily, in research.

21
00:01:57,000 --> 00:02:05,000
 And so we, in some sense, even though the war is a terrible thing, we sort of benefit from the extensive research.

22
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:09,000
 They were trying to solve wartime problems, but they ultimately solved problems

23
00:02:09,000 --> 00:02:12,000
 that have changed our peacetime world in wonderful ways.

24
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:19,000
 So Bletchley Park is north of London between Oxford and Cambridge in England,

25
00:02:19,000 --> 00:02:24,000
 and it was a code-breaking effort, and at one point there were over 10,000 people

26
00:02:24,000 --> 00:02:34,000
 working on top-secret efforts to decode encrypted messages initially from Germans as they were using radio.

27
00:02:34,000 --> 00:02:42,000
 So they call World War II a "World War," mostly because it touched geographically more than any other war

28
00:02:42,000 --> 00:02:47,000
 had ever touched before or since, really.

29
00:02:47,000 --> 00:02:57,000
 You know, Alaska, United States, Italy, Africa, Russia, Japan, Philippines,

30
00:02:57,000 --> 00:03:05,000
 you know, it truly was geographically distributed, and it was necessary to do unprecedented communications

31
00:03:05,000 --> 00:03:12,000
 just to effectively pursue the war, and this meant that communications had to be wireless.

32
00:03:12,000 --> 00:03:17,000
 And the problem with wireless is anyone can put up an antenna and listen to the wireless signal.

33
00:03:17,000 --> 00:03:22,000
 There's no way to hide the wireless signal, unlike a wire that you could hide it,

34
00:03:22,000 --> 00:03:25,000
 and if no one had access to the wire, you can't see what's in it.

35
00:03:25,000 --> 00:03:29,000
 But if you're using wireless and fast-moving armies in long distances,

36
00:03:29,000 --> 00:03:33,000
 then someone can intercept the wireless signal, and they can.

37
00:03:33,000 --> 00:03:36,000
 So the trick, of course, was to create an encrypted wireless signal,

38
00:03:36,000 --> 00:03:39,000
 so they could see everything you sent, but it didn't make any sense to them.

39
00:03:39,000 --> 00:03:42,000
 It all sounded like gibberish, unless you, of course, know the code.

40
00:03:42,000 --> 00:03:47,000
 And so this was a key technology was building codes and code-making machines.

41
00:03:47,000 --> 00:03:51,000
 A good example of this was the Enigma made by Germany,

42
00:03:51,000 --> 00:03:55,000
 which is -- we'll talk about these in this last part, very last part of the class,

43
00:03:55,000 --> 00:03:58,000
 in the security part of the class, how these codes and cypers work,

44
00:03:58,000 --> 00:04:03,000
 but they scramble material in a way that's generally unintelligible.

45
00:04:03,000 --> 00:04:06,000
 And, of course, the really bright folks at Bletchley Park --

46
00:04:06,000 --> 00:04:11,000
 Alan Turing, one of many really bright people at Bletchley Park --

47
00:04:11,000 --> 00:04:17,000
 used, you know, mathematics to say, you know, these codes may be more crackable than we think.

48
00:04:17,000 --> 00:04:22,000
 There was folks from Poland who also informed them to say,

49
00:04:22,000 --> 00:04:26,000
 look, let's show what we did to crack it right before Poland was involved.

50
00:04:26,000 --> 00:04:29,000
 They were working heavily on the mathematics of cracking.

51
00:04:29,000 --> 00:04:32,000
 And so then they built these machines.

52
00:04:32,000 --> 00:04:35,000
 And so in this video that I'm about to show you,

53
00:04:35,000 --> 00:04:39,000
 it's really -- what I tried to do is I tried to contrast the two machines,

54
00:04:39,000 --> 00:04:45,000
 and one is an extremely fast mechanical computer with relays and switches

55
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:49,000
 and things that spin and gears that move back and forth.

56
00:04:49,000 --> 00:04:53,000
 It's a very physical computer that's looking for patterns.

57
00:04:53,000 --> 00:04:57,000
 And as it's spinning, it's checking for possible encoding combinations

58
00:04:57,000 --> 00:05:02,000
 to try to just do brute force checking of lots of different possibilities.

59
00:05:02,000 --> 00:05:05,000
 And so it was a mechanical computer.

60
00:05:05,000 --> 00:05:07,000
 The Bomba was a mechanical computer.

61
00:05:07,000 --> 00:05:09,000
 And then as the German encryption improved

62
00:05:09,000 --> 00:05:12,000
 and they used different techniques with more sophisticated encryption,

63
00:05:12,000 --> 00:05:15,000
 they just couldn't decrypt it with a mechanical computer anymore.

64
00:05:15,000 --> 00:05:18,000
 And so they just were forced to build something faster.

65
00:05:18,000 --> 00:05:23,000
 And that faster thing was the first truly powerful

66
00:05:23,000 --> 00:05:26,000
 general purpose electronic computer in the world.

67
00:05:26,000 --> 00:05:28,000
 Of course, it was kept secret until the --

68
00:05:28,000 --> 00:05:30,000
 certainly kept secret until the '60s and the '70s,

69
00:05:30,000 --> 00:05:33,000
 and much of it was still kept secret until even the '90s.

70
00:05:33,000 --> 00:05:39,000
 And so its place in history is kind of a recent understanding.

71
00:05:39,000 --> 00:05:42,000
 You can look at early history texts that talk about the first computer,

72
00:05:42,000 --> 00:05:43,000
 and they don't mention this one.

73
00:05:43,000 --> 00:05:46,000
 Well, that's because it was a secret until a long time.

74
00:05:46,000 --> 00:05:49,000
 So the Bomba was a powerful mechanical computer.

75
00:05:49,000 --> 00:05:52,000
 The Colossus was a powerful electronic computer.

76
00:05:52,000 --> 00:05:56,000
 But I had this picture that was drawn by an artist for me.

77
00:05:56,000 --> 00:06:02,000
 And in addition to showcasing sort of the moment where a mechanical computer,

78
00:06:02,000 --> 00:06:05,000
 no matter how hard you tried, wouldn't work fast enough,

79
00:06:05,000 --> 00:06:10,000
 the electronic computer was sort of forcefully created out of tremendous need.

80
00:06:10,000 --> 00:06:14,000
 What's also really interesting is the fact that Bletchley Park,

81
00:06:14,000 --> 00:06:19,000
 during this time with 10,000 people, had all kinds of people --

82
00:06:19,000 --> 00:06:25,000
 language experts, mathematicians, engineers, welders.

83
00:06:25,000 --> 00:06:28,000
 And it's a really cross-disciplinary activity.

84
00:06:28,000 --> 00:06:33,000
 And they were solving a problem of decrypting German transmissions,

85
00:06:33,000 --> 00:06:38,000
 but they ultimately solved the problem in the pursuit of that,

86
00:06:38,000 --> 00:06:42,000
 of electronic communications and computation.

87
00:06:42,000 --> 00:06:48,000
 And so this picture is really trying to show how Alan Turing was really very critical.

88
00:06:48,000 --> 00:06:51,000
 But there were other people like Gordon Welchman and Doc Kean

89
00:06:51,000 --> 00:06:56,000
 and the folks from Poland that informed all of this, university colleagues.

90
00:06:56,000 --> 00:07:02,000
 And this whole thing was very much a connected, collective group of really bright people,

91
00:07:02,000 --> 00:07:07,000
 highly motivated, well-funded, and they created this.

92
00:07:07,000 --> 00:07:15,000
 So let me go ahead and pause now and let you take a look at this film.

